The most common adverse event was nausea (generally mild-to-moderate). Discontinuation due to adverse events was 6.7%.\n*   **Celvara:** Demonstrated a favorable safety profile compared to the active control standard-of-care. The SAE rate was 7.3% (vs. 8.9% for methotrexate). There were zero treatment-related deaths. The primary adverse events were localized mild-to-moderate injection site reactions. Hepatic safety outperformed the methotrexate control. Discontinuation due to adverse events was 8.1%.\n*   **Zyphranol:** Exhibited considerable safety signals. The SAE rate was 14.2% (vs. 6.1% placebo). There were three treatment-related deaths in the study arm (two cardiac, one hepatic failure). \n    *   *Cardiovascular:* Major Adverse Cardiovascular Events (MACE) occurred at a rate of 2.8% (vs. 0.9% placebo, Hazard Ratio: 3.24). A post-hoc subgroup analysis noted a 5.1% MACE rate in patients over 65 years old.\n    *   *Hepatic:* Significant ALT elevations (>3x ULN) occurred in 8.7% of patients (vs. 1.2% placebo).\n    *   *Infections:* Serious infections requiring hospitalization occurred in 4.1% of patients.\n    *   *Discontinuations:* Due to these adverse events, 18.3% of Zyphranol patients discontinued the trial, notably above the class norm.\n\n## FDA Advisory Communication on Zyphranol\n\nThe FDA Division of Rheumatology and Inflammation reviewed the Phase III safety data for Zyphranol. The FDA highlighted that the 14.2% SAE rate, the cardiovascular safety signal (MACE HR 3.24), the hepatotoxicity trends, and the 18.3% discontinuation rate diverge negatively from typical averages for this indication class. To address these safety risks, the FDA has mandated that VitaNova Therapeutics provide 6-month post-trial cardiac safety follow-up data, an independent adjudication of hepatic events, and an updated benefit-risk assessment prior to Zyphranol's advisory committee meeting in Q2 2026."})
