l efficacy among the three\ntherapies, with a 26 percentage-point improvement over placebo in ACR50\nresponse and 31% DAS28-CRP remission. However, its safety profile presents\nsubstantial concerns: the SAE rate (14.2%) is more than double the placebo\nrate and well above the class average, three treatment-related deaths\noccurred (two cardiovascular, one hepatic), the MACE hazard ratio of 3.24\nconstitutes a statistically significant cardiovascular signal (particularly\nin patients >65, where MACE reached 5.1%), hepatotoxicity affected 8.7% of\npatients with one fatal case, and the 18.3% discontinuation rate far exceeds\nthe typical range for this drug class. The FDA has formally requested\nadditional safety data before advisory committee review.\n\nRivamod showed robust efficacy (24 pp ACR50 advantage over placebo) with a\nsafety profile closely approximating placebo across all major categories,\nincluding no treatment-related deaths, no cardiovascular or hepatic signals,\nand a low discontinuation rate (6.7%).\n\nCelvara demonstrated modest but statistically significant superiority over\nmethotrexate (the standard-of-care active comparator), with a favorable\nsafety profile including lower hepatic toxicity than methotrexate and no\ntreatment-related deaths. Its efficacy advantage over the active comparator\nwas smaller in magnitude than the placebo-controlled differences seen with\nZyphranol and Rivamod, though direct cross-trial comparison is not possible.\n\nIn summary, while Zyphranol shows the strongest efficacy signals, its\nbenefit-risk profile requires careful evaluation given the significant\nsafety findings. Rivamod offers a strong efficacy–safety balance. Celvara\nprovides a modest improvement over existing standard of care with a favorable\nsafety and tolerability profile.\n"})
