se Events:\n    Injection site reactions: Celvara 12.4% (mild-moderate, self-resolving)\n    — erythema 8.1%, pruritus 4.3%\n    Upper respiratory infections: Celvara 9.8% vs Methotrexate 11.2%\n\n  Hepatic Safety:\n    ALT elevations >3× ULN: Celvara 1.8% vs Methotrexate 5.4%\n    Celvara demonstrated a more favorable hepatic profile than MTX.\n\n  Trial Discontinuation Due to Adverse Events:\n    Celvara 8.1% (62/762) vs Methotrexate 10.4% (79/762)\n\n================================================================================\nCROSS-TRIAL COMPARATIVE OVERVIEW\n================================================================================\n\n                         Zyphranol    Rivamod     Celvara\n                         (vs PBO)     (vs PBO)    (vs MTX)\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nACR50 Response           47%          42%         39%\nACR50 Δ vs Control       +26 pp       +24 pp      +7 pp\nDAS28-CRP Remission      31%          28%         24%\nHAQ-DI Change            −0.58        −0.51       −0.42\nSAE Rate                 14.2%        5.9%        7.3%\nTreatment-Related Deaths 3            0           0\nMACE Rate                2.8%         0.6%        N/R*\nALT >3× ULN              8.7%         1.1%        1.8%\nSerious Infections       4.1%         1.9%        N/R*\nAE Discontinuation       18.3%        6.7%        8.1%\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nPBO = placebo; MTX = methotrexate; N/R* = not reported as a\nstandalone category in the trial report (Celvara used active\ncomparator design); pp = percentage points\n\n================================================================================\nKEY TAKEAWAYS\n================================================================================\n\n1. EFFICACY: Zyphranol demonstrated the highest absolute ACR50 response\n   rate (47%) and the largest treatment vs. control difference (+26 pp)\n   among the three agents. Rivamod showed comparable efficacy (42% ACR50,\n   +24 pp). Celvara showed modest but statistically significant superiority\n   over methotrexate (+7 pp).\n\n2. SAFETY — CRITICAL DISTINCTIONS: The safety profiles differ substantially\n   across the three agents:\n\n   a) Zyphranol carries significant safety concerns:\n      - SAE rate (14.2%) more than double the class average (6–8%)\n      - Three treatment-related deaths (2 cardiac, 1 hepatic failure)\n      - Statistically significant MACE signal (HR 3.24, 95% CI 1.47–7.15)\n        with pronounced risk in patients >65 years (5.1% MACE)\n      - Clinically notable hepatotoxicity (8.7% ALT >3× ULN; 1 fatal case)\n      - Suspected PML case in the context of serious infections\n      - 18.3% discontinuation rate due to adverse events\n      The FDA has flagged these findings and requested additional data\n      before advisory committee review.\n\n   b) Rivamod demonstrated a safety profile closely approximating placebo:\n      - SAE rate near placebo (5.9% vs 5.4%)\n      - Zero treatment-related deaths\n      - No cardiovascular or hepatic signals\n      - No opportunistic infections\n      - Discontinuation rate (6.7%) within the typical class range\n\n   c) Celvara showed a favorable safety profile relative to methotrexate:\n      - SAE rate lower than active control (7.3% vs 8.9%)\n      - Zero treatment-related deaths\n      - Superior hepatic profile vs. methotrexate (1.8% vs 5.4% ALT >3× ULN)\n      - Discontinuation rate lower than active control (8.1% vs 10.4%)\n      - Primary AEs were mild injection site reactions\n\n3. BENEFIT-RISK CONSIDERATIONS: While Zyphranol shows the strongest\n   efficacy signal, its safety profile raises substantial concerns that\n   may limit its use, particularly in patients over 65. The FDA has\n   requested additional cardiac safety data and hepatic event adjudication\n   before proceeding to advisory committee review. Rivamod and Celvara\n   both offer more favorable safety profiles, with Rivamod showing\n   near-placebo tolerability and efficacy comparable to Zyphranol, and\n   Celvara offering a safety advantage over the current standard of care\n   (methotrexate) albeit with more modest efficacy.\n\n================================================================================\nDATA SOURCES\n================================================================================\n\n- VitaNova Therapeutics, Phase III Trial Report (NCT05847291), data cutoff\n  September 30, 2025\n- Aethon Pharmaceuticals, Phase III Trial Report (NCT05719402), data cutoff\n  October 12, 2025\n- Meridian Biosciences, Phase III Trial Report (NCT05623187), data cutoff\n  August 15, 2025\n- FDA Division of Rheumatology and Inflammation, Pre-Decision Review\n  Communication, November 3, 2025\n================================================================================\n'})
