or Adverse Cardiovascular Events (MACE):\n    Zyphranol 2.8% (26/926) vs Placebo 0.9% (8/921); HR 3.24 (95% CI 1.47–7.15).\n    Age-stratified analysis showed MACE of 5.1% in patients >65 years vs 1.1%\n    in the placebo subgroup (interaction p=0.003). The FDA has identified this\n    as a statistically significant cardiovascular safety signal and noted it\n    may warrant a boxed warning if confirmed.\n\n  Hepatotoxicity:\n    ALT elevations >3× ULN: Zyphranol 8.7% (81/926) vs Placebo 1.2% (11/921).\n    Grade 3–4 hepatic events: 2.1% vs 0.2%. The fatal hepatic failure case\n    underscores this risk. The FDA has mandated additional hepatic event\n    adjudication by an independent committee.\n\n  Serious Infections Requiring Hospitalization:\n    Zyphranol 4.1% (38/926) vs Placebo 1.8% (17/921), including 2 cases of\n    opportunistic infection (1 suspected PML).\n\n  Trial Discontinuation Due to Adverse Events:\n    Zyphranol 18.3% (170/926) vs Placebo 5.2% (48/921). This discontinuation\n    rate substantially exceeds the typical range for this drug class (6–10%).\n\nFDA ADVISORY STATUS\n  The FDA Division of Rheumatology has formally requested (a) 6-month\n  post-trial cardiac safety follow-up data, (b) independent hepatic event\n  adjudication, and (c) an updated benefit-risk assessment before the\n  scheduled Q2 2026 advisory committee meeting.\n\n================================================================================\n2. RIVAMOD (RVM-305) — Aethon Pharmaceuticals\n================================================================================\nDesign: Randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, 52-week study\nEnrollment: N=2,103 (1,052 Rivamod / 1,051 placebo); 156 global sites\nRegistration: NCT05719402\n\nEFFICACY ENDPOINTS\n\n  Primary — ACR50 Response at Week 24:\n    Rivamod 42% vs Placebo 18% (absolute difference +24 pp, 95% CI 20.1–27.9,\n    p<0.001).\n\n  Secondary — DAS28-CRP Remission at Week 52:\n    Rivamod 28% vs Placebo 7% (p<0.001).\n\n  Patient-Reported — HAQ-DI Change from Baseline:\n    Rivamod -0.51 (SD 0.40) vs Placebo -0.15 (SD 0.36), exceeding the\n    clinically meaningful improvement threshold of -0.22.\n\nSAFETY AND ADVERSE EVENTS\n\n  Serious Adverse Events (SAEs):\n    Rivamod 5.9% (62/1,052) vs Placebo 5.4% (57/1,051). Near-parity with\n    placebo.\n\n  Treatment-Related Deaths:\n    Zero in both arms.\n\n  Most Common Adverse Events:\n    Nausea: Rivamod 15.2% vs Placebo 4.8% (mild-to-moderate).\n    Headache: Rivamod 8.3% vs Placebo 7.1%.\n\n  Cardiovascular Safety:\n    MACE: Rivamod 0.6% vs Placebo 0.5%. No cardiovascular signal detected by\n    independent DSMB review.\n\n  Hepatic Safety:\n    ALT elevations >3× ULN: Rivamod 1.1% vs Placebo 0.9%. No hepatic safety\n    signal identified.\n\n  Serious Infections:\n    Rivamod 1.9% vs Placebo 1.5%. No opportunistic infections reported.\n\n  Trial Discontinuation Due to Adverse Events:\n    Rivamod 6.7% (70/1,052) vs Placebo 4.9% (52/1,051).\n\n================================================================================\n3. CELVARA (CLV-220) — Meridian Biosciences\n================================================================================\nDesign: Randomized, double-blind, active-controlled (vs methotrexate), 48-week\nEnrollment: N=1,524 (762 Celvara / 762 methotrexate); 98 sites\nRegistration: NCT05623187\n\nNote: Celvara was studied against an active comparator (methotrexate) rather\nthan placebo, making direct efficacy comparisons with the other two agents\nimprecise.\n\nEFFICACY ENDPOINTS\n\n  Primary — ACR50 Response at Week 24:\n    Celvara 39% vs Methotrexate 32% (absolute difference +7 pp, 95% CI 2.3–\n    11.7, p=0.018).\n\n  Secondary — DAS28-CRP Remission at Week 48:\n    Celvara 24% vs Methotrexate 19% (p=0.021).\n\n  Patient-Reported — HAQ-DI Change from Baseline:\n    Celvara -0.42 (SD 0.39) vs Methotrexate -0.35 (SD 0.37), exceeding the\n    clinically meaningful improvement threshold of -0.22.\n\nSAFETY AND ADVERSE EVENTS\n\n  Serious Adverse Events (SAEs):\n    Celvara 7.3% (56/762) vs Methotrexate 8.9% (68/762). Favorable relative\n    to active comparator.\n\n  Treatment-Related Deaths:\n    Zero in both arms.\n\n  Most Common Adverse Events:\n    Injection site reactions: Celvara 12.4% (mild-moderate, self-resolving;\n    erythema 8.1%, pruritus 4.3%).\n    Upper respiratory infections: Celvara 9.8% vs Methotrexate 11.2%.\n\n  Hepatic Safety:\n    ALT elevations >3× ULN: Celvara 1.8% vs Methotrexate 5.4%. Celvara\n    demonstrated a more favorable hepatic profile than methotrexate.\n\n  Trial Discontinuation Due to Adverse Events:\n    Celvara 8.1% (62/762) vs Methotrexate 10.4% (79/762).\n\n================================================================================\n4. COMPARATIVE OVERVIEW\n================================================================================\n\nEFFICACY (ACR50 at Week 24):\n  Zyphranol demonstrated the highest absolute ACR50 response (47%) and the\n  largest placebo-adjusted difference (+26 pp). Rivamod followed at 42%\n  (+24 pp vs placebo). Celvara achieved 39% versus methotrexate (32%),\n  with a modest +7 pp difference. Direct head-to-head comparisons are not\n  possible due to differing trial designs and patient populations.\n\n  DAS28-CRP remission rates followed a similar rank order: Zyphranol 31%,\n  Rivamod 28%, Celvara 24% (vs MTX 19%).\n\nSAFETY:\n  Zyphranol's safety profile raises significant concerns. Its SAE rate\n  (14.2%) is more than double that of Rivamod (5.9%) and substantially\n  exceeds Celvara (7.3%). Three treatment-related deaths occurred in the\n  Zyphranol arm (2 cardiac, 1 hepatic), while no treatment-related deaths\n  were reported for Rivamod or Celvara. The MACE hazard ratio of 3.24 for\n  Zyphranol represents a clear cardiovascular signal, whereas Rivamod and\n  Celvara showed no such signal. Hepatotoxicity was also markedly more\n  frequent with Zyphranol (8.7% ALT >3× ULN) compared with