[INFO] Executing: create_file({'file_path': '/summary.txt', 'content': "PHASE III CLINICAL TRIAL SUMMARY: CHRONIC INFLAMMATORY ARTHRITIS\n================================================================\n\nThree Phase III trials were reviewed for emerging therapies in moderate-to-severe\nchronic inflammatory arthritis: Zyphranol (VitaNova Therapeutics), Rivamod\n(Aethon Pharmaceuticals), and Celvara (Meridian Biosciences). An FDA advisory\ncommunication regarding Zyphranol safety was also reviewed. Summary follows.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. EFFICACY ENDPOINTS\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nPRIMARY ENDPOINT — ACR50 Response at Week 24:\n  Zyphranol:  47% vs Placebo 21% — absolute difference 26pp (p<0.001)\n  Rivamod:    42% vs Placebo 18% — absolute difference 24pp (p<0.001)\n  Celvara:    39% vs MTX     32% — absolute difference  7pp (p=0.018)\n\n  Note: Zyphranol and Rivamod were placebo-controlled; Celvara was\n  active-controlled against methotrexate. Direct cross-trial comparisons\n  should be made with caution due to differing designs and populations.\n  Zyphranol achieved the highest absolute ACR50 response rate.\n\nSECONDARY ENDPOINT — DAS28-CRP Remission (Week 48–52):\n  Zyphranol:  31% vs Placebo  9% (p<0.001)\n  Rivamod:    28% vs Placebo  7% (p<0.001)\n  Celvara:    24% vs MTX     19% (p=0.021)\n\nPATIENT-REPORTED OUTCOMES — HAQ-DI Change from Baseline:\n  Zyphranol:  -0.58 (SD 0.41) vs Placebo -0.19\n  Rivamod:    -0.51 (SD 0.40) vs Placebo -0.15\n  Celvara:    -0.42 (SD 0.39) vs MTX     -0.35\n  Clinically meaningful improvement threshold: -0.22. All three drugs\n  exceeded this threshold in the treatment arms.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2. SAFETY AND ADVERSE EVENTS\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSERIOUS ADVERSE EVENTS (SAEs):\n  Zyphranol:  14.2% (132/926)  vs Placebo 6.1%   — 2.3× placebo rate\n  Rivamod:     5.9% (62/1,052) vs Placebo 5.4%   — near placebo parity\n  Celvara:     7.3% (56/762)   vs MTX     8.9%   — favorable vs active control\n\n  Zyphranol's SAE rate substantially exceeds both its placebo arm and the\n  class average for approved biologic therapies in this indication (6–8%),\n  as noted in the FDA Division of Rheumatology's preliminary review.\n\nTREATMENT-RELATED DEATHS:\n  Zyphranol:  3 deaths (2 cardiac events, 1 acute hepatic failure)\n  Rivamod:    0 deaths\n  Celvara:    0 deaths\n\n  The three deaths in the Zyphranol arm are a significant safety concern.\n  The FDA has flagged these for careful causal evaluation.\n\nCARDIOVASCULAR SAFETY (MACE):\n  Zyphranol:  2.8% vs Placebo 0.9% — HR 3.24 (95% CI: 1.47–7.15)\n  Rivamod:    0.6% vs Placebo 0.5% — no signal per DSMB\n  Celvara:    Not reported as a signal vs MTX\n\n  Zyphranol shows a statistically significant cardiovascular signal. In the\n  post-hoc age-stratified analysis, patients >65 years had a MACE rate of\n  5.1% vs 1.1% placebo (interaction p=0.003). The FDA advisory communication\n  notes this may warrant a BOXED WARNING if confirmed.\n\nHEPATOTOXICITY (ALT >3× ULN):\n  Zyphranol:  8.7% vs Placebo 1.2% (Grade 3–4: 2.1% vs 0.2%)\n  Rivamod:    1.1% vs Placebo 0.9%\n  Celvara:    1.8% vs MTX     5.4%\n\n  Zyphranol's hepatotoxicity rate is markedly elevated. The fatal hepatic\n  failure case further underscores this concern. The FDA has indicated\n  mandatory liver function monitoring may be required. In contrast, Celvara\n  demonstrated a more favorable hepatic profile than methotrexate.\n\nSERIOUS INFECTIONS REQUIRING HOSPITALIZATION:\n  Zyphranol:  4.1% vs Placebo 1.8% — including 2 opportunistic infections\n              (1 suspected progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy)\n  Rivamod:    1.9% vs Placebo 1.5% — no opportunistic infections\n  Celvara:    Not a reported signal\n\n  The suspected PML case in the Zyphranol arm is of particular clinical\n  concern given the known class risk for certain immunomodulatory agents.\n\nTRIAL DISCONTINUATION DUE TO ADVERSE EVENTS:\n  Zyphranol:  18.3% (170/926)  vs Placebo 5.2%\n  Rivamod:     6.7% (70/1,052) vs Placebo 4.9%\n  Celvara:     8.1% (62/762)   vs MTX     10.4%\n\n  Zyphranol's discontinuation rate of 18.3% substantially exceeds the\n  typical range for this drug class (6–10%), as flagged by the FDA,\n  and may impact real-world effectiveness.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3. REGULATORY STATUS\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe FDA Division of Rheumatology has issued a preliminary advisory\ncommunication specific to Zyphranol, requesting the following before the\nQ2 2026 advisory committee meeting:\n\n  (a) 6-month post-trial cardiac safety follow-up data for all participants\n  (b) Detailed hepatic event adjudication by an independent committee\n  (c) Updated benefit-risk assessment incorporating the above data\n\nNo similar regulatory holds or requests have been noted for Rivamod or\nCelvara in the materials reviewed.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n4. OVERALL BENEFIT-RISK ASSESSMENT\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nEFFICACY: Zyphranol demonstrated the highest efficacy across all endpoints,\nwith an ACR50 response of 47%, DAS28-CRP remission of 31%, and a HAQ-DI\nimprovement of -0.58 — all statistically significant vs placebo. Rivamod\nshowed comparable but slightly lower efficacy (ACR50 42%, DAS28-CRP 28%),\nand Celvara demonstrated modest superiority over methotrexate (ACR50 39%\nvs 32%).\n\nSAFETY: The safety profiles diverge substantially. Zyphranol carries\nsignificant safety concerns including a 14.2% SAE rate, 3 treatment-related\ndeaths, a statistically significant cardiovascular signal (MACE HR 3.24),\nclinically meaningful hepatotoxicity (8.7% ALT >3×