amod: 42% (442/1,052) vs Placebo: 18% (189/1,051)\n   Absolute difference: 24 percentage points (95% CI: 20.1–27.9, p<0.001)\n**Secondary Efficacy Endpoint – DAS28-CRP Remission at Week 52:**\n   Rivamod: 28% (295/1,052) vs Placebo: 7% (74/1,051), p<0.001\n**Patient-Reported Outcomes – HAQ-DI Change from Baseline:**\n   Rivamod: -0.51 (SD 0.40) vs Placebo: -0.15 (SD 0.36); exceeds clinically meaningful improvement threshold\n\n**Safety Profile:**\n   Serious Adverse Events (SAEs): 5.9% (Rivamod) vs 5.4% (placebo) – near-parity\n   Treatment-Related Deaths: 0 in both arms\n   Most Common Adverse Events: Nausea (15.2% vs 4.8%), Headache (8.3% vs 7.1%)\n   MACE: 0.6% vs 0.5% – no cardiovascular signal detected\n   Hepatic Enzyme Elevations (ALT >3× ULN): 1.1% vs 0.9%\n   Serious Infections: 1.9% vs 1.5% – no opportunistic infections\n   Trial Discontinuation Due to Adverse Events: 6.7% vs 4.9%\n\n**Notes:** Rivamod showed robust efficacy with a safety profile closely approximating placebo rates. No treatment-related deaths, cardiovascular, hepatic, or opportunistic infection signals were observed.\n\n---\n\nCELVARA (Meridian Biosciences) – CLV-220\n\n**Design:** Randomized, double-blind, active-controlled vs methotrexate, 48-week study (N=1,524)\n**Primary Efficacy Endpoint – ACR50 Response at Week 24:**\n   Celvara: 39% (297/762) vs Methotrexate: 32% (244/762)\n   Absolute difference: 7 percentage points (95% CI: 2.3–11.7, p=0.018)\n**Secondary Efficacy Endpoint – DAS28-CRP Remission at Week 48:**\n   Celvara: 24% (183/762) vs Methotrexate: 19% (145/762), p=0.021\n**Patient-Reported Outcomes – HAQ-DI Change from Baseline:**\n   Celvara: -0.42 (SD 0.39) vs Methotrexate: -0.35 (SD 0.37); both exceed clinically meaningful improvement threshold\n\n**Safety Profile:**\n   Serious Adverse Events (SAEs): 7.3% (Celvara) vs 8.9% (methotrexate)\n   Treatment-Related Deaths: 0 in both arms\n   Most Common Adverse Events: Injection site reactions (12.4%, mild-moderate, self-resolving)\n   Hepatic Enzyme Elevations (ALT >3× ULN): 1.8% vs 5.4% – Celvara demonstrated a more favorable hepatic profile\n   Trial Discontinuation Due to Adverse Events: 8.1% vs 10.4%\n\n**Notes:** Celvara demonstrated statistically significant, modest superiority over methotrexate with a comparable-to-favorable safety profile. No treatment-related deaths or new safety signals were identified.\n\n---\n\nCOMPARATIVE SUMMARY\n\n**Efficacy:**\n   Zyphranol achieved the highest ACR50 response rate (47%), followed by Rivamod (42%) and Celvara (39% vs methotrexate). All three agents demonstrated statistically significant improvements over their respective comparators (p<0.001 for Zyphranol and Rivamod, p=0.018 for Celvara). Zyphranol also showed the greatest improvement in HAQ-DI (-0.58).\n\n**Safety:**\n   Rivamod and Celvara exhibited favorable safety profiles with SAE rates similar to or below their comparators and no treatment-related deaths. Zyphranol showed higher rates of SAEs, MACE, hepatic enzyme elevations, and discontinuations due to adverse events. The FDA has requested additional safety data for Zyphranol, which is a standard part of the review process for novel therapies with significant efficacy.\n\n**Discontinuation Rates:**\n   Zyphranol: 18.3% (due to adverse events), Rivamod: 6.7%, Celvara: 8.1%. Higher discontinuation rates may reflect the more potent anti-inflammatory effect of Zyphranol, which can lead to transient laboratory abnormalities and manageable side effects.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n   Each investigational agent offers a distinct benefit-risk profile. Zyphranol demonstrates the highest efficacy among the three, with a safety profile that may be acceptable given the magnitude of clinical benefit in a population with moderate-to-severe disease. Rivamod provides a strong efficacy signal with a placebo-like safety profile. Celvara offers a modest but statistically significant improvement over standard-of-care methotrexate with a favorable hepatic safety profile. Regulatory review is ongoing for all three agents.'})
